<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Tupou I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1797–1893</span> · Also known as Tāufaʻāhau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Tupou I, born Tāufaʻāhau, was a chief from Haʻapai who converted to Wesleyan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and united Tonga through civil wars, ruling as king from 1845. He issued law codes, freed commoners from forced labour to chiefs and granted the constitution of 1875, which still underpins the monarchy. His kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enele-maafu">Enele Maʻafu</a> built Tongan power in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i.jpg" alt="George Tupou I" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Tupou_I,_c._1880s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
